From 8169c0151f7d083c11d159afe0401510616a3b00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: xob0t <32616886+xob0t@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 11:41:57 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Switches through byte-indexed jump tables: decode both
 tables, switch on the case value

MSVC compiles a switch whose many case values share few bodies into two tables: a byte per case value giving the
index of its body, then the table of the bodies' offsets from the image base:
    cmp eax, N ; ja default ; lea rdx, [image base]
    movzx eax, byte [rdx + rax + bytes] ; mov ecx, [rdx + rax*4 + targets] ; add rcx, rdx ; jmp rcx
The jump-table reader took the bound of the "cmp" (the number of case values) as the length of the table of
targets, read past its end, found the count wrong and left the jump an indirect tail call: the case bodies were
never decoded and the method ended at "__call_indirect(...)" with the table load as an opaque value.

The reader now sees that the index of the target load was last written by a "movzx" of a byte from an
image-relative table, reads one byte per case value and returns the targets per case value; the lifter switches on
the byte table's index (the case value) rather than on the byte, working out the constants the builder folded into
the address ("value - 430 + bytes" is one constant). REWIND_NO_BYTE_JUMP_TABLES turns this off.

The register a jump table jumps through holds a code address in every case. It was left with the target's value,
so where a case body calls a method whose arguments are not known yet the table load stayed alive as an argument
and as an opaque statement ("_ = __load4(table + i*4) + base") in an otherwise complete method. It is undefined
after the jump now; REWIND_NO_DEAD_JUMP_TARGETS keeps it.

On a commercial Unity 6000.0.60f1 game all 33 byte-indexed tables of the binary and its 292 one-level tables in
methods give the native targets case by case; 10 more bodies are complete (7 in Assembly-CSharp, among them a
menu's button actions and the factory of a level's objects), none lost or changed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
